Falsifies `paging::x86_64::the_two_halves_are_disjoint`.

The defect: a mask-and-compare where an equality belongs. `x & M == x` asks whether `x` FITS in
`M`, not whether it equals it, and the two read almost identically at a glance; the first is
what you write to test a set of flags and the second is what this line means. `top` is `va >>
47`, which is at most seventeen bits, and `u64::MAX >> 47` is seventeen ones, so the test is
true for every address ever passed to it. The high half swallows the whole address space, and
with it every check that says "refuse this, it is a kernel address".

Expected to fail: `assert!(!(Ia32e::is_in_half(Half::Low, va) && Ia32e::is_in_half(Half::High, va)))`,
for any low-half address at all, `va == 0` included. That it fails so widely is the point: this
defect makes the high-half test say nothing, and only a proof that quantifies over both tests at
once notices, since each one alone still answers questions.

**The half test is shared and the proof is not.** The `aarch64` record makes the low half an
inclusive range and the `sv39` one moves the sign bit.

diff --git a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
--- a/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
+++ b/crates/paging/src/lib.rs
@@ -488,7 +488,7 @@ pub trait PageFormat {
         let top = va >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT;
         match half {
             Half::Low => top == 0,
-            Half::High => top == (u64::MAX >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT),
+            Half::High => top & (u64::MAX >> Self::SPLIT_SHIFT) == top,
         }
     }
 
